## Architecture and Current Behavior

- The frontend creates each download UUID before invoking Rust and creates the popup state first. Rust uses that UUID for every event. A bounded frontend pending-event map protects against exceptionally early events.
- URL preview has a 450 ms debounce. Only one backend probe task remains active; a new probe aborts the previous oEmbed/yt-dlp task. YouTube/Vimeo oEmbed is described only as metadata availability, not full yt-dlp format validation.
- `.ru`/`.рф` domains require the VPN decision before thumbnail, oEmbed, or yt-dlp probing. Approval is cached only for the same normalized URL.
- URLs that may contain a collection show an explicit `Лише це відео` / `Уся добірка` choice. Single is the default and always sends `--no-playlist`; collection mode sends `--yes-playlist` and pacing options. Extractors can also reveal the choice after preview by returning `itemCount > 1`, which is how a Threads video carousel becomes selectable without treating every single Threads post as a collection.
- The preflight is an invisible, single-click safety step: it is network-free, reuses preview duration/item count plus local disk data, and immediately starts the download when space is sufficient. Playlist estimates use the already-summed duration when every entry has one; otherwise they scale a representative item duration by item count without a second network request. There is no preflight card or second confirmation click. Size/free-space details belong in the active job popup; the live disk guard remains authoritative.
- Preview uses a 450 ms debounce and a 4 s soft deadline. If it still cannot be verified, the UI explains that availability could not be determined quickly and permits a download attempt.
- Starting a job clears the URL field. Successful completion keeps it empty; confirmed user cancellation restores the exact URL and playlist intent so the user can change the destination and retry immediately.
- The URL field has a dedicated one-click clear control. The sidebar version is read from `package.json.releaseVersion`, the deferred Settings item is hidden, and Download/History are real navigable views.
- The Download header communicates broad support in one concise line: video, audio, and subtitles from YouTube, Instagram, Twitter, and almost anywhere else; it deliberately avoids presenting a redundant service checker or publicly naming Russian platforms.
- Every terminal job emits each final converted output path and exact on-disk size it actually produced, including partial results created before a later item fails or is cancelled. The frontend stores those real outputs (but never an output-less failure/cancellation) in up to 500 local history records with source metadata, exact download settings, and thumbnail locations; groups them by collapsible local calendar date; and rechecks file presence/size every 10 seconds while History is open. Missing files and disconnected-volume paths remain visible as unavailable.
- History thumbnails are downloaded in the background only when a terminal job produced at least one real final output. They live in the app-local `history-thumbnails` directory, survive offline use, fall back to the original HTTPS URL if a cached file becomes unavailable, and are removed when History is cleared. Cache/write/clear/delete operations are serialized, and a late cache result cleans itself up when its history entry was cleared without deleting a thumbnail shared by another entry. The cache accepts only raster image MIME types, rejects files above 8 MiB, retains at most 550 files, rejects private/loopback/link-local/special IP ranges, pins DNS resolution for each request, revalidates every redirect, and is exposed through a narrowly scoped Tauri asset protocol.
- Persisted history is parsed through a strict runtime normalizer: invalid dates or missing required identity/source/path fields are dropped per record, while safe legacy/partial records receive bounded defaults for sizes, availability, and every retry setting. Availability polling reads current entries through a ref, including replacements at the 500-entry limit, and preserves state identity when file status did not change.
- `Завантажити ще раз` restores the original URL, destination, video/audio mode, quality, audio format, subtitle flag, and single/collection intent. It falls back to the system Downloads directory if the old destination disappeared, runs the normal quick safety probe, and then starts automatically.
- Unknown estimates remain visibly unknown. Start is allowed only above the runtime guard threshold, and a live guard checks free space during yt-dlp, merge, video FFmpeg, and audio FFmpeg.
- The runtime guard triggers at 628 MiB free (500 MiB protected reserve plus 128 MiB write buffer), kills the active process group, preserves completed/user source files, and removes only the app-owned FFmpeg temporary output.
- Video downloads that require compatibility processing are merged to an intermediate file and converted by a managed FFmpeg process to controlled-bitrate 8-bit `yuv420p` H.264/AAC MP4 with real percent, speed, and ETA. A selected hardware encoder gets exactly one real attempt and then one `libx264` fallback on both macOS and Windows. Encoder selection is cached for the current FFmpeg file/version so a queue does not repeat hardware probes for every item. A ready single-file H.264/AAC MP4 bypasses MKV, merge, and app-managed FFmpeg regardless of its website; codec metadata is used when present and a fast managed-ffprobe stream check is the fallback. `--merge-output-format mkv` remains enabled but yt-dlp ignores it when no merge is required; universal `--remux-video mkv` is deliberately absent.
- Audio downloads keep the source first, then the app runs managed FFmpeg for MP3, M4A, Opus, or WAV with real percent, speed, and ETA. The source is preserved if conversion fails.
- Runtime assets stream directly to temporary files without a total transfer timeout, emit install progress, verify SHA-256 from disk, and expose per-component Retry without an app restart. Runtime maintenance is globally serialized, runs component checks sequentially behind a blocking cross-view modal, refuses to overlap an active download, and keeps the previous executable available until its replacement succeeds. Maintenance timestamps are written only after success.
- Windows uses managed `yt-dlp.exe`, Deno, and `yt-dlp/FFmpeg-Builds` FFmpeg/FFprobe. Child commands use `CREATE_NO_WINDOW`; cancellation kills the complete Windows process tree; disk checks use the cross-platform filesystem API; cookie login defaults to Edge; `.ru` route checks resolve the active Windows interface through `Find-NetRoute`.
- A restrictive Tauri CSP permits local scripts/styles, IPC, local assets, and HTTPS thumbnail images; it does not allow remote scripts or `unsafe-eval`.

